   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Kokoda Initiative Partnership supported health facilities and workers in Kokoda to improve health outcomes. Australia supported three health workers to attend training on HIV/AIDS provided by the PNG National Department of Health (NDoH). The training increased knowledge on HIV transmission, prevention and treatment, along with counselling for those affected. 
&#x200d;
NDoH Maternal Child Health (MCH) Patrols, supported by Australia, conducted antenatal care for expectant mothers, and provided advice on family planning. Patrols also administered routine immunisation for children under five. For example, in early 2024, MCH patrols in Central Province reported 3,446 immunisations, including 424 for infants under the age of one. 
&#x200d;
Health facility inspections confirmed that previously provided assistance, including a microscope for tuberculosis screening, was being maintained and in use. Further, the inspection demonstrated that the facility was being managed appropriately and indicated that health workers are implementing procedures to detect and prevent the spread of infectious diseases.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The program strengthened health systems and contributed to improved health service seeking behaviour. Support included clinical training for health workers and Village Health Volunteers, regular Maternal and Child Health Patrols, and a significant refurbishment of the Kokoda Memorial Hospital, co-financed by provincial authorities. 
&#x200d;
Health initiatives were delivered in partnership with provincial authorities and contributed to improved antenatal coverage (85%), childhood immunisation rates (80%), and access to family planning (80%), as well as decreased maternal mortality rates. Integrated outreach patrols reached 2329 men and 2649 women during the reporting period, and 46 health workers received clinical training.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening leadership</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the Kokoda Initiative Partnership (KIP), Australia is working with the PNG Government to strengthen local leadership in environmental conservation and heritage protection. A key focus has been building the capacity of the Kokoda Track Authority (KTA) rangers&#x2014;both men and women&#x2014;who play a vital role in managing and preserving the Kokoda Track.
&#x200d;
With Australian support, six rangers (three men and three women) successfully completed an internationally recognised 18-month Certificate III in Conservation and Ecosystems Management. This training, part of the Ranger Capacity Development Program (RCDP), included a unique ranger exchange program with Indigenous Australian rangers from Carpentaria Land Council Aboriginal Corporation, promoting cross-cultural learning and collaboration in land management.
&#x200d;
Australia supported women&#x2019;s participation in traditionally male-dominated conservation roles. In October 2024, two rangers (1M:1W) presented their achievements on the world stage at the 10th International Ranger Federation World Congress in France, showcasing PNG&#x2019;s success in ranger training and leadership development.
&#x200d;
Additionally, Kokoda Initiative Partnership supported 60 Flexible Open Distance Education (FODE) scholarships (33M:27W) for teachers and health workers along the Track enhancing local capacity and leadership in service delivery. Together, these initiatives are empowering communities with the skills and confidence to lead conservation and development efforts along the Kokoda Track.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">With KIP support, the KTA progressed drafting legislation for the Kokoda Track Management Authority (KTMA), which will improve the PNG Government&#x2019;s capacity to manage the Track. A zero draft was finalised and is being modified following community feedback. 
&#x200d;
Nineteen (15M:4F) KTA rangers received internationally recognised Conservation and Ecosystems Management training, with 12 (9M:3F) progressing to advanced training. The Rangers enhanced the KTA&#x2019;s ability to manage the reopening of the Track and maintain safety infrastructure. They also assisted with events, dispute resolution, and community consultations.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
